CORE
JmlSignalsOnly
--function "JmlSignalsOnly.safeSqrt:(I)I" --jml-source src/main/java/JmlSignalsOnly.java --unwind 5 -cp target/classes
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR
^.*FAILURE$
--
Regression for `signals_only T1, T2, ...;` JML clauses.

The method actually throws IllegalArgumentException for
negative inputs; the contract permits exactly that type to
escape. JBMC's signals_only lowering must:

  1. Parse the comma-separated type-name list (handling the
     `signals_only` prefix without confusing it with
     `signals`).
  2. At every method exit, ASSERT that the inflight exception
     is null OR matches one of the listed types via
     @class_identifier comparison (post-remove_instanceof
     form, since JML lowering runs after remove_exceptions
     and remove_instanceof).
  3. Resolve simple class names to fully-qualified Java type
     identifiers — `IllegalArgumentException` matches
     `java::java.lang.IllegalArgumentException` even though
     the user wrote the unqualified name.
  4. Include all subclasses transitively in the allowed-set.
  5. ASSIGN @inflight_exception := NULL after the assertion
     so the harness's default no-uncaught-exception check
     still passes (signals_only declares which exceptions
     are *permitted* to escape, not that they must propagate).
  6. Retarget GOTOs that previously jumped directly to
     END_FUNCTION (e.g. exception-throwing paths from
     remove_exceptions) so they reach the inserted check
     rather than skipping past it.
